UxOeHc2bWZoTUdCN2JmR0trYW02Zm9vem83WS00Z3dCRjBLMjhoemJ1dXFqNjdOR0g4bXZ2SHRrSWUtRUZucTlBTld4WnJVbzVsY1RZWEhwa2UtdDlHZkZuRkJhSmkwbjdZWko2OVVFckgxckFkbUlneHpidUVxOFZ3WFpIQ2xGRTA?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": -0.45, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": 3.05, "poll_sd": 4.52, "poll_weight": 0.55, "fundamentals": {"margin": -2.37, "lean": -8.0, "environment": 7.83, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": 0.8}, "rating_margin": 0.83, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.61, "baseline_margin": 1.48, "analyst_adjustment": 1.02, "key_factors": ["Late polls consistently show Hamilton ahead by 3-5 points in a state Trump carried twice", "Hamilton's fundraising advantage ($19.2M vs $5.4M) with no outside opposition spending", "Raters split between Tossup and Lean D, with several flip ratings in the final days", "Trump's repeated Kansas rallies signal GOP concern about Marshall's standing"], "rationale": "Recent high-quality polls (NYT/Siena, YouGov, Emerson, SSRS) all show Hamilton leading by 3-5 points, and prediction markets price him near 62%. The fundamentals still favor Republicans in a red state, so the forecast lands at a modest Democratic edge rather than a larger one.", "watch": "Whether the apparent late Democratic surge holds through Election Day or reflects differential polling participation, as the NYT itself cautions.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Roger Marshall (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kansas has become an unexpected Senate battleground. Incumbent Roger Marshall, renominated despite modest fundraising, faces Adam Hamilton, who has outraised him roughly three to one and holds a large cash advantage. Final polls from high-quality pollsters uniformly show Hamilton ahead by three to five points, though some outlets caution that late Democratic surges can overstate true standing. Prediction markets put Hamilton's chances near 62 percent.\n\nExpert raters are split, with most calling the race a Tossup and several moving to Lean D in the final days. Trump ha